INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('426d043a62121fcd5671','Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf','41','Dermatology','eee
Jane, 53 years, presents to your GP practice with facial weakness, right ptosis, and drooling from the right corner of her mouth since this morning. She mentions a history of right ear','{"A":"few days earlier. On examination, an erythematous vesicular rash is noted in the right ear canal. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate next step in\nmanagement?\n\nv OA. Oral aciclovir.","B":"Oral prednisone.","C":"Methotrexate.","D":"Clindamycin.","E":"Gabapentin."}','A','The clinical findings and history suggest Ramsay Hunt syndrome as the most likely diagnosis. The syndrome is defined as an acute peripheral facial neuropathy caused by the
reactivation of a latent varicella-zoster virus infection. The syndrome is associated with an erythematous vesicular rash of the skin of the ear canal, auricle (also known as herpes
zoster oticus), and/or oropharyngeal mucous membrane.

Varicella zoster virus (VZV) infection causes two distinct clinical syndromes. Primary infection, also known as varicella or chickenpox, is a common pediatric viral disease. After a
chickenpox infection, VZV remains latent in the neurons of cranial nerves and dorsal root ganglia. Subsequent reactivation of latent VZV can result in a vesicular rash in a dermatomal

distribution, known as herpes zoster. VZV infection or reactivation involving the geniculate ganglion of the 7 cranial nerve (CN VII) within the temporal bone is the main
pathophysiological mechanism of Ramsay-Hunt syndrome.

Ramsay-Hunt syndrome is estimated to account for 16% of all causes of unilateral facial palsies in children, and 18% of facial palsies in adults. Ramsay Hunt syndrome is thought to
be the cause of as many as 20% of clinically diagnosed cases of Bell palsy. Although VZV reactivation is the cause in most cases of this syndrome, herpes simplex virus (HSV) II has
been implicated in some cases.

The syndrome usually presents with paroxysmal pain deep within the ear. The pain often radiates outward into the pinna of the ear and may be associated with a more constant,
diffuse, and dull background pain. The onset of pain usually precedes the rash by several hours or even days.

The classic triad includes (1) ipsilateral facial palsy, (2) ear pain, and (3) vesicles in the auditory canal and auricle. In some patients taste perception, hearing (tinnitus, hyperacusis),
and lacrimation may occur.

Ramsay-Hunt syndrome is generally considered a polycranial neuropathy, with frequent involvement of cranial nerves V, IX, and X. With the involvement of the geniculate ganglion,

the inflammation can spread to the 8" cranial nerve (CN VIII), resulting in auditory and vestibular disorders.
Clinical manifestations of Ramsay Hunt syndrome may include:

¢ Vesicular rash of the ear or mouth (as many as 80% of cases)

¢ The rash might precede the onset of facial paresis/palsy (involvement of the seventh cranial nerve [CN VII])
¢ Ipsilateral lower motor neuron facial paresis/palsy (CN VII)

¢ Vertigo and ipsilateral hearing loss (CN VIII)

¢ Tinnitus

¢ Hyperacusis

* Otalgia (ear pain)

* Headaches

¢ Dysarthria

¢ Gait ataxia

¢ Fever

* Cervical lymphadenopathy

¢ Facial weakness usually reaches maximum severity by one week after the onset of symptoms.

¢ Other cranial neuropathies might be present and may involve cranial nerves (CNs) VIII, IX, X, V, and VI.
¢ Ipsilateral hearing loss has been reported in as many as 50% of cases.

¢ Blisters of the skin of the ear canal, auricle, or both may become secondarily infected, causing cellulitis.

Antiviral treatment should be started in all patients within 72 hours of the onset of the rash. This time limit does not apply to patients with herpes zoster ophthalmicus and
immunocompromised patients, meaning that such patients should receive antiviral therapy even beyond 72 hours of presentation.

Treatment options include famciclovir, valaciclovir, and aciclovir.
NOTE - Aciclovir is the preferred medication in children and pregnant women.

For this patient, oral acyclovir is the most appropriate option to consider as the next step in management.

(Option A) Therapy with oral corticosteroids (e.g. prednisolone daily for 7 days, tapering to 5 mg daily over the next 2 weeks) can reduce symptoms in the acute (inflammatory) phase
of herpes zoster, but only when used in combination with an antiviral agent. Corticosteroids are recommended for patients over the age of 50 if not contraindicated and should be
used with caution in patients with comorbid conditions such as diabetes.

(Option C) Methotrexate has no role in the treatment of herpes zoster.
(Option D) Antibiotics other than mentioned antiviral agents are ineffective in treating herpes zoster.

(Option E) Gabapentin and tricyclic antidepressants are the most effective treatment options for neuropathic pain associated with herpes zoster infection, but given the time of
presentation, antiviral therapy should be considered as the cornerstone of therapy first.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-91b61a30981f","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"426d043a62121fcd5671","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":41,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"426d043a62121fcd5671","source_file":"Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":41,"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Dermatology","stem":"eee\nJane, 53 years, presents to your GP practice with facial weakness, right ptosis, and drooling from the right corner of her mouth since this morning. She mentions a history of right ear","options":{"A":"few days earlier. On examination, an erythematous vesicular rash is noted in the right ear canal. Which one of the following would be the most appropriate next step in\nmanagement?\n\nv OA. Oral aciclovir.","B":"Oral prednisone.","C":"Methotrexate.","D":"Clindamycin.","E":"Gabapentin."},"source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"The clinical findings and history suggest Ramsay Hunt syndrome as the most likely diagnosis. The syndrome is defined as an acute peripheral facial neuropathy caused by the\nreactivation of a latent varicella-zoster virus infection. The syndrome is associated with an erythematous vesicular rash of the skin of the ear canal, auricle (also known as herpes\nzoster oticus), and/or oropharyngeal mucous membrane.\n\nVaricella zoster virus (VZV) infection causes two distinct clinical syndromes. Primary infection, also known as varicella or chickenpox, is a common pediatric viral disease. After a\nchickenpox infection, VZV remains latent in the neurons of cranial nerves and dorsal root ganglia. Subsequent reactivation of latent VZV can result in a vesicular rash in a dermatomal\n\ndistribution, known as herpes zoster. VZV infection or reactivation involving the geniculate ganglion of the 7 cranial nerve (CN VII) within the temporal bone is the main\npathophysiological mechanism of Ramsay-Hunt syndrome.\n\nRamsay-Hunt syndrome is estimated to account for 16% of all causes of unilateral facial palsies in children, and 18% of facial palsies in adults. Ramsay Hunt syndrome is thought to\nbe the cause of as many as 20% of clinically diagnosed cases of Bell palsy. Although VZV reactivation is the cause in most cases of this syndrome, herpes simplex virus (HSV) II has\nbeen implicated in some cases.\n\nThe syndrome usually presents with paroxysmal pain deep within the ear. The pain often radiates outward into the pinna of the ear and may be associated with a more constant,\ndiffuse, and dull background pain. The onset of pain usually precedes the rash by several hours or even days.\n\nThe classic triad includes (1) ipsilateral facial palsy, (2) ear pain, and (3) vesicles in the auditory canal and auricle. In some patients taste perception, hearing (tinnitus, hyperacusis),\nand lacrimation may occur.\n\nRamsay-Hunt syndrome is generally considered a polycranial neuropathy, with frequent involvement of cranial nerves V, IX, and X. With the involvement of the geniculate ganglion,\n\nthe inflammation can spread to the 8\" cranial nerve (CN VIII), resulting in auditory and vestibular disorders.\nClinical manifestations of Ramsay Hunt syndrome may include:\n\n¢ Vesicular rash of the ear or mouth (as many as 80% of cases)\n\n¢ The rash might precede the onset of facial paresis/palsy (involvement of the seventh cranial nerve [CN VII])\n¢ Ipsilateral lower motor neuron facial paresis/palsy (CN VII)\n\n¢ Vertigo and ipsilateral hearing loss (CN VIII)\n\n¢ Tinnitus\n\n¢ Hyperacusis\n\n* Otalgia (ear pain)\n\n* Headaches\n\n¢ Dysarthria\n\n¢ Gait ataxia\n\n¢ Fever\n\n* Cervical lymphadenopathy\n\n¢ Facial weakness usually reaches maximum severity by one week after the onset of symptoms.\n\n¢ Other cranial neuropathies might be present and may involve cranial nerves (CNs) VIII, IX, X, V, and VI.\n¢ Ipsilateral hearing loss has been reported in as many as 50% of cases.\n\n¢ Blisters of the skin of the ear canal, auricle, or both may become secondarily infected, causing cellulitis.\n\nAntiviral treatment should be started in all patients within 72 hours of the onset of the rash. This time limit does not apply to patients with herpes zoster ophthalmicus and\nimmunocompromised patients, meaning that such patients should receive antiviral therapy even beyond 72 hours of presentation.\n\nTreatment options include famciclovir, valaciclovir, and aciclovir.\nNOTE - Aciclovir is the preferred medication in children and pregnant women.\n\nFor this patient, oral acyclovir is the most appropriate option to consider as the next step in management.\n\n(Option A) Therapy with oral corticosteroids (e.g. prednisolone daily for 7 days, tapering to 5 mg daily over the next 2 weeks) can reduce symptoms in the acute (inflammatory) phase\nof herpes zoster, but only when used in combination with an antiviral agent. Corticosteroids are recommended for patients over the age of 50 if not contraindicated and should be\nused with caution in patients with comorbid conditions such as diabetes.\n\n(Option C) Methotrexate has no role in the treatment of herpes zoster.\n(Option D) Antibiotics other than mentioned antiviral agents are ineffective in treating herpes zoster.\n\n(Option E) Gabapentin and tricyclic antidepressants are the most effective treatment options for neuropathic pain associated with herpes zoster infection, but given the time of\npresentation, antiviral therapy should be considered as the cornerstone of therapy first.","source_answer_status":"present","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"high","currentness_keywords":["age","antibiotic","diabetes","management","pregnan","therapy","treatment"],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"91b61a30981f7b002f3e13eddac49d96b41d2b8b","duplicate_group_id":"exact-91b61a30981f","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"426d043a62121fcd5671","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Dermatology amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":41,"source_qid":null}]}',1,'v2.9.4','2026-09-24T00:00:00.000Z') ON CONFLICT(record_id) DO UPDATE SET source_file=excluded.source_file,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,stem=excluded.stem,options_json=excluded.options_json,source_answer=excluded.source_answer,source_explanation=excluded.source_explanation,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,visual_asset=excluded.visual_asset,visual_url=excluded.visual_url,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,current_answer_status=excluded.current_answer_status,current_evidence_json=excluded.current_evidence_json,currentness_risk=excluded.currentness_risk,record_json=excluded.record_json,active_quiz=excluded.active_quiz,import_version=excluded.import_version,imported_at=excluded.imported_at;
