INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('6cb194cc4df1fcf0cf86','Git amedex 2025.pdf','29','Gastroenterology','A 66-year-old man presents with intermittent right upper quadrant pain. An ultrasound, performed to reveal the
cause of gallstones, reveals a lesion in the liver. A triple-phase CT scan is performed for more evaluation,
showing a 35 mm subcortical lesion with early prominent dense enhancement, which spreads through the lesion
in the late portal venous phase. Which one of the following conditions would fit this description best?','{"A":"A. Isolated metastatic lesion.","B":"OB. Hemangioma.","C":"C. Hepatocellular carcinoma.","D":"D. Hepatic cyst.","E":"E. Hydatid cyst."}','B','The patient has a provisional diagnosis of biliary colic, most likely due to biliary stones. The ultrasound scan has
been used to confirm the diagnosis as the best initial diagnostic tool. Furthermore, any associated inflammation
would be evaluated.

Inflammation of the gallbladder manifests as thickening of the gallbladder wall and the presence of
pericholecystic fluid. Stones might also be seen in the common bile duct; however, ultrasound''s sensitivity for
detecting ductal stones is low (30%—50%).

As a routine procedure, when scanning for biliary problems, the sonographer will also scan the liver. In this case,
the sonographer has encountered an incidental finding irrelevant to the presenting symptoms, for which a
triphasic CT scan of the liver has been performed.

Early prominent dense enhancement of the lesion during the arterial phase is characteristic of liver hemangioma
(the most common benign liver tumor). Hemangiomas are seen in approximately 20% of the general population.
They may be solitary or multiple. The lesions typically show intense enhancement during the arterial phase of the
triphasic CT scan and retain a blush of contrast during the portal venous phase.

(Options A and C) Most malignant liver tumors (primary or metastatic) are hypovascular and will not have early
enhancement during the arterial phase; rather, they become more pronounced during the portal venous phase.

(Option D) Cystic lesions in the liver may be simple, multiple (polycystic liver disease), neoplastic, or infective
(hydatid cysts). Simple cysts are extremely common and usually asymptomatic. On imaging, they have a low-
density homogenous appearance. With polycystic disease, the number and size of the cysts often lead to
symptoms.

(Option E) Hydatid cysts have a characteristic septate appearance and heterogenous appearance if they contain
daughter cysts. Liver abscesses are usually symptomatic and more likely to have a heterogenous appearance.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[29],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-351c7d78c2d9","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"6cb194cc4df1fcf0cf86","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":29,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"332ce55a4c8616b7a2b6","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_page":78,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','low','{"record_id":"6cb194cc4df1fcf0cf86","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":29,"supporting_pages":[29],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology","stem":"A 66-year-old man presents with intermittent right upper quadrant pain. An ultrasound, performed to reveal the\ncause of gallstones, reveals a lesion in the liver. A triple-phase CT scan is performed for more evaluation,\nshowing a 35 mm subcortical lesion with early prominent dense enhancement, which spreads through the lesion\nin the late portal venous phase. Which one of the following conditions would fit this description best?","options":{"A":"A. Isolated metastatic lesion.","B":"OB. Hemangioma.","C":"C. Hepatocellular carcinoma.","D":"D. Hepatic cyst.","E":"E. Hydatid cyst."},"source_answer":"B","source_explanation":"The patient has a provisional diagnosis of biliary colic, most likely due to biliary stones. The ultrasound scan has\nbeen used to conf